{
  "summary": "The previous proposal's incremental extraction plan (helpers first, then free-function action modules) is pragmatically sound but underweights three structural risks that could make the refactor stall mid-way. First, the 'free async functions that accept a PipelineApp instance' pattern for action_* methods is essentially a God Object anti-pattern in disguise — passing the entire PipelineApp as a parameter means every extracted function still has implicit access to all 30+ reactive attributes, the server client, config, lock state, and every private method, so the coupling is unchanged and the only benefit is fewer lines in one file rather than genuine decoupling. A better extraction boundary would define a narrow ActionContext protocol/dataclass exposing only what actions actually need (selected_feature, active_board, config, a notify callback, a push_screen callback, and a refresh callback) — this forces each action to declare its dependencies explicitly and makes testing possible without instantiating a full Textual App. Second, the proposal dismisses inotify/watchdog too quickly. The claim that 'the real cost is redundant processing, not stat syscalls' is wrong at scale: _check_for_changes (line 2749) calls _load_features which walks board directories and instantiates FeatureFile objects, then stats each one, then builds a snapshot list, then diffs against previous — this is O(n) work on every polling cycle regardless of whether anything changed. A hash over (path, mtime) tuples only optimizes the comparison step, not the directory walk and object construction that dominates. With inotify/watchdog, the entire polling loop becomes event-driven and the refresh path only fires when something actually changes — this is a fundamentally different cost model, not an incremental optimization. Third, none of the proposals address the elephant in the room: the _run_plan_async and _run_implement_async methods (lines 3906 and 4045) are 100+ line async workflows that orchestrate multi-step agent runs with error handling, retry logic, lock management, and state transitions — these are the most complex and bug-prone parts of PipelineApp and they belong in a dedicated orchestration module (which partially exists already as pipeline.py and phases.py). Extracting UI helpers while leaving the orchestration core untouched inverts the priority: the helpers are read-only and stable, while the orchestration methods are where all the bugs and feature additions concentrate."
}
